# COVERAGE FIXTURE. Deliberate test data, not an ontology about anything.
#
# RULE: cls-hv1
# W3C OWL 2 Web Ontology Language Profiles (Second Edition), section 4.3,
# Table 6: The Semantics of Classes.
# https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-profiles/#Reasoning_in_OWL_2_RL_and_RDF_Graphs_using_Rules
#
#   T(?x, owl:hasValue, ?y)
#   T(?x, owl:onProperty, ?p)
#   T(?u, rdf:type, ?x)
#     ->  T(?u, ?p, ?y)
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. No document in this repository asserts owl:hasValue, so
# tools/horn_differential.py compared the Rust and Python engines on cls-hv1
# zero times and its clean sweep said nothing about it. That is worth closing
# for this rule in particular: src/reason.rs records that the arm emitted under
# this name was once the COMPOSITE of cax-sco and W3C cls-hv1, demanding a
# subclass step the standard rule does not ask for. A rule that has already been
# wrong once, and that no corpus document exercises, is the weakest row in the
# table.
#
# EXPECTED: exactly one derivation, credited to cls-hv1:
#   :x :p :v
#
# cls-hv2 then fires on that conclusion and re-derives ":x rdf:type :R", which
# is asserted, so it adds no step and takes no credit.

@prefix : <http://ex.org/cover/cls-hv1#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

:R a owl:Restriction ;
   owl:onProperty :p ;
   owl:hasValue :v .

:x a :R .
